# ProofOfIntent -- Semantic Checkout Firewall

PayPal AI Hackathon project. POI sits between an AI shopping agent's PayPal order and the capture.
It re-reads the order from PayPal, checks it against the user's signed IntentSpec, then captures or voids.
This side of the repo builds everything that attacks POI and shows it: the Adversarial Bazaar,
the shopping agent, the web app and the benchmark.

## Rules

- Read docs/CONTRACTS.md before any change. Schemas in packages/shared are the source of truth --
  import them from "@poi/shared"; never redefine a contract type locally.
- Layer 2 (Immune): read docs/CONTRACTS-L2.md first. I also own packages/arena, apps/bazaar/src/arena,
  apps/web/app/{arena,immune,calibration} and bench/evolved. Implement packages/arena to the
  `ArenaEngine` interface in @poi/shared (l2.ts) exactly; build pages against `pnpm mock:api`
  (fixtures in @poi/shared/fixtures-l2, a fake attacker in @poi/shared/arena-fake).
- I own apps/web, apps/bazaar, packages/agent, bench/, postman/. Do not edit apps/api, apps/mcp,
  packages/shared, prisma/ (ask Parvati first).
- Changing a shared schema/endpoint/event: stop and tell me -- my teammate depends on it.
- TypeScript strict, ESM, Node 20+. Zod-validate request bodies and data files.
- Money: PayPal amounts are strings ("29.99"). Use toCents/fromCents from @poi/shared for math, never floats.
- Use the typed POI client (createPoiClient from @poi/shared). Until Tue evening it points at the mock
  API: run `pnpm mock:api` (or `pnpm dev:mock` for web + bazaar + mock together).
- LLM models are "provider:model" ids resolved with getModel from "@poi/shared/llm" (server-side only).
- Attacks are realistic: fictional brands only, attacks live in descriptions/reviews/specs/alt text,
  every poisoned listing must be a genuinely wrong purchase for its scenario.
- The shopping agent's prompt stays a normal helpful assistant -- no defensive instructions.
- Never commit secrets; use .env (gitignored) and keep .env.example complete.
- Before committing: pnpm typecheck && pnpm test.
